<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kroraina</h1><p class="st-meta">176 BCE – 445 CE (approx.) · capital Kroraina (Loulan), Yuni</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of Loulan, later Shanshan, on the shores of Lop Nur, whose desert-preserved documents give an unusually detailed view of Silk Road life.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kroraina, called Loulan in Chinese, was an oasis state on the north-western shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lop-nur">Lop Nur</a> in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, the first stop for travellers leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> westward. It appears in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> letter to the Han of 176 BCE. In 77 BCE the Han envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-jiezi">Fu Jiezi</a> assassinated its pro-Xiongnu king, and the Han installed Weituqi, renaming the kingdom Shanshan and moving its centre south to Yuni.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the 3rd and 4th centuries Shanshan used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandhari-language">Gandhari Prakrit</a> in Kharoshthi script for government; hundreds of wooden tablets found at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/niya">Niya</a> record taxes, lawsuits, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and slaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> flourished, and its monasteries and paintings show Indian and Hellenistic influence. Chinese garrisons were stationed at Loulan, and its kings balanced between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the steppe.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by the drying of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lop-nur">Lop Nur</a> and wars with its neighbours, Shanshan was conquered by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> in 445. The ruins of Loulan and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/niya">Niya</a> were rediscovered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sven-hedin">Sven Hedin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurel-stein">Aurel Stein</a> around 1900.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kroraina.jpg" alt="Kroraina" width="320" height="164"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LoulanCarvedWoodenBeam.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>176 BCE – 445 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kroraina (Loulan), Yuni</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Weituqi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gandhari Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Loulan, Shanshan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=134&amp;focus=kroraina" aria-label="Kroraina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6885 730 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kroraina lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7205 986L7444 753M7205 986L7769 1191M7205 986L7771 1192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7444 753h0M7769 1191h0M7771 1192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7205 986h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7205 986h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=134&amp;focus=kroraina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

